-- allium: 3
-- rotation-daemon.allium

-- Scope: Credential rotation daemon for Bitwarden
-- Includes:
--   Daemon startup (creating the transport) and server connection lifecycle
--   Work acquisition: claiming server-notified jobs (ClaimAvailableRotation) -- the
--     daemon's side of the pull model; job/attempt state stays server-side
--   Rotation execution (one try at a rotation), running the integration for the
--     target-system kind (Entra, MSSQL, CustomScript) carried on the claim
--   Verification of the rotated credential against the target system before the
--     rotation is reported succeeded, and reporting the vault<->target sync state a
--     failed try leaves behind (sync_state on the failure report)
--   Resolving target-system connection details from daemon-local config, keyed by the
--     target system's id. The server defines the target system and holds NO connection
--     details (see rotation-server.allium / TargetSystem); the daemon supplies them.
--   Best-effort session termination as a guidance-level step of executing a rotation,
--     with its own local retry budget (a termination failure never re-runs the rotation).
-- Excludes:
--   Bitwarden server internals (external system)
--   Bitwarden Secrets Manager internals (external system)
--   Password generation algorithm internals
--   Deployment and containerisation (Docker, etc.)
--   Rotation scheduling, job/claim orchestration, the RotationAttempt record, and retry
--     orchestration across attempts -- all server-side (rotation-server.allium). The
--     daemon is a pure client: it claims a notified job (ClaimAvailableRotation), receives
--     a self-contained work snapshot in answer, executes a single try against it, and
--     reports the outcome back to the server (which owns the RotationAttempt). A retry is
--     a fresh claim against the same server-side job, answered with a fresh snapshot. The
--     daemon holds NO attempt entity of its own.

config {
    -- The daemon's small, local retry budget for absorbing TRANSIENT glitches within
    -- a single rotation -- a brief network blip or a momentary target-system error --
    -- so a temporary fault does not bubble up as a reported failure. It covers the
    -- target-integration and cipher calls during a rotation (see ExecuteRotation) and
    -- the session-termination loop (also ExecuteRotation guidance). This is deliberately
    -- small and is DISTINCT from the server's attempt-level retry: a hard failure that
    -- survives these local retries is reported to the server, which makes the job
    -- claimable again for a fresh attempt (rotation-server.allium / RetryJob).
    max_retry_attempts: Integer = 5
    retry_base_delay: Duration = 1.second
}

-- External entities managed by Bitwarden

external entity Cipher {
    id: String
    username: String
    password: String
}

-- The work snapshot the daemon executes against (the password policy, target details,
-- cipher, account identity, terminate flag) is owned server-side and carried on each claim
-- (rotation-server.allium / RotationClaimed) as opaque snapshot parameters. The daemon
-- holds no typed copy of these shapes: it cannot reference server types (the one-way
-- layering) and keeps no attempt entity, so it simply forwards them through its execution
-- triggers (StartRotation -> ExecuteRotation) and reports the outcome by attempt_id. The
-- password policy in particular is server-owned configuration on the TargetSystem
-- (rotation-server.allium / PasswordPolicy); the daemon receives it and generates a
-- conforming password without modelling its shape locally.

-- Target systems are defined and owned server-side (rotation-server.allium / TargetSystem):
-- the server holds the id, name, rotation method, kind and password policy, but NO
-- connection details. When a rotation is claimed, the work snapshot the daemon needs to
-- act is carried to it on the claim (rotation-server.allium / RotationClaimed -> the
-- StartRotation / ExecuteRotation trigger parameters below); the daemon resolves the actual
-- connection details (host, username, password, ...) from a pluggable resolver keyed by the
-- target system's id (see CredentialResolver). The entra | mssql | custom_script kind
-- selects which integration the daemon runs. The daemon holds NO attempt entity: it works
-- only from the snapshot handed to it on each claim and reports the outcome by attempt_id.

-- account_identity is an opaque String: the account's identifier in the target system,
-- interpreted by the target's kind (an Entra UPN/email, a SQL Server login, a custom-script
-- argument). There is no type or kind discriminator on it -- the integration selected by
-- kind knows how to read it. It rides on the claim snapshot below and is never parsed here.

-- Server connection
-- The transport: the daemon's (WebSocket-like) link to the Bitwarden server. This is the
-- link lifecycle only -- establishing it, losing it, reconnecting -- and is separate from
-- DaemonSession, the authenticated session (access token, organization key, refresh and
-- revoke) that rides on top of an established connection. The link can drop and reconnect
-- many times within the life of one credential, and the access token expires and refreshes
-- independently of the link. A transient drop (disconnected) reconnects with backoff; a
-- connection whose authentication is rejected is terminally refused -- the credential is
-- no good, so the daemon stops rather than reconnecting forever against it (a replacement
-- credential must be provisioned; see the credential re-issuance open question).
--
-- SignalR/WebSockets owns the raw transport: socket setup, keep-alive, and reconnection
-- with backoff are the library's job, not the daemon's. This entity models only the
-- outline the daemon's own logic depends on (am I connected? authenticated? do I need to
-- re-auth or stop?), deliberately not the connection mechanics.

entity ServerConnection {
    status: connecting | connected | disconnected | refused

    transitions status {
        connecting -> connected
        connecting -> refused
        connected -> disconnected
        connected -> refused
        disconnected -> connecting
        terminal: refused
    }
}

-- Authentication
-- The daemon authenticates with a provisioned access token exactly as a Secrets Manager
-- machine account does: one string of the form 0.<client_id>.<client_secret>:<encryption_key>.
-- The half before the colon is an OAuth2 client-credentials grant (client_id + client_secret)
-- the server exchanges for a short-lived bearer access token; the segment AFTER the colon is
-- a symmetric encryption_key that never leaves the daemon. At registration the server wrapped
-- the organization key under that encryption_key and stored only the wrapped blob
-- (rotation-server.allium / DaemonRegistration.encrypted_organization_key); on each
-- authentication it returns that blob, and the daemon unwraps it locally with encryption_key
-- to recover the org key. The daemon needs the org key to encrypt password updates into
-- organization vault ciphers; the server never holds the encryption_key, the plaintext org
-- key, or any plaintext password (zero-knowledge). This is SM's exact token + key-delivery
-- mechanic; the only difference is that the daemon's authorization to write vault ciphers is
-- broader than an SM machine account's -- the residual crypto blast radius is the "Read blast
-- radius" open question, contained at the API layer (rotation-server.allium / ApiScopedReads).

external entity DaemonCredential {
    client_id: String
    client_secret: String
    -- The post-colon segment of the access token: a symmetric key that unwraps the org key
    -- returned on authentication. Lives only on the daemon; never transmitted to the server.
    encryption_key: String
}

-- The daemon operates with a single provisioned credential.
given {
    credential: DaemonCredential
}

-- Unwrap the organization key the server returns on authentication (the wrapped blob
-- rotation-server.allium / DaemonRegistration.encrypted_organization_key) using the daemon's
-- own encryption_key. Local, daemon-only crypto: the encryption_key never leaves the daemon,
-- so the server cannot perform this itself (zero-knowledge).
deferred UnwrapOrganizationKey    -- see: daemon-local org-key unwrap with credential.encryption_key

-- The authenticated session that rides on a ServerConnection: it holds the short-lived
-- access token and the decrypted organization key, and tracks the credential lifecycle
-- (authenticate -> active, expire -> refresh, or revoke). Distinct from ServerConnection,
-- which is just the transport: one session is established per connection attempt
-- (DaemonConnects), and the connection is only signalled ready once this session
-- authenticates (HandleAuthenticationSucceeded -> OpenConnection).
entity DaemonSession {
    id: String
    connection: ServerConnection
    credential: DaemonCredential
    -- closed: the session's transport dropped (ConnectionDropClosesSessions). Terminal,
    -- like revoked, but semantically distinct: the credential is still good, and the
    -- reconnected transport gets a FRESH session (DaemonConnects) -- a dropped session is
    -- never resumed. Closing the old session on the drop is what upholds
    -- AtMostOneActiveSession across reconnects.
    status: authenticating | active | expired | revoked | closed
    access_token: String when status = active
    expires_at: Timestamp when status = active
    organization_key: String when status = active

    transitions status {
        authenticating -> active
        authenticating -> revoked
        authenticating -> closed
        active -> expired
        active -> revoked
        active -> closed
        expired -> authenticating
        expired -> closed
        terminal: revoked, closed
    }
}

-- Deferred helpers

-- Resolve the opaque connection details for a target system (host, login, secret,
-- per-kind connection material), keyed by its id. The DEFAULT resolver is
-- environment-variable lookup (e.g. "<id>_HOST", "<id>_USER", "<id>_SECRET"); Bitwarden
-- Secrets Manager and a local config file are pluggable, opt-in resolvers that can be
-- configured to take priority. The lookup, precedence, per-kind shape and the resolved
-- credential value are all implementation concerns, encapsulated here -- the server holds
-- none of this; only the daemon does. Referenced from ExecuteRotation in place of any
-- inline priority logic.
deferred CredentialResolver    -- see: daemon-local credential resolution (default: environment variables; pluggable: Bitwarden Secrets Manager, local config)

-- Actors

-- The Bitwarden server as the party on the far side of the daemon's transport. It is
-- code, not a person; identified minimally by the connection the daemon holds to it --
-- any connection that has not been terminally refused may carry server-originated events.
actor BitwardenServer {
    identified_by: ServerConnection where status != refused
}

-- The target-system integration the daemon runs (Entra Graph, a SQL Server, a custom
-- script). It is code, not a person; identified nominally by the daemon's active session,
-- under which all rotation execution happens. The actual target it talks to is selected by
-- the kind on the work snapshot carried on the claim, not by a local entity.
actor Integration {
    identified_by: DaemonSession where status = active
}

-- Surfaces

surface DaemonLifecycle {
    -- The daemon process boundary: whoever runs the daemon (an operator, a service
    -- manager, a container runtime) starts it with its provisioned credential. Starting
    -- the daemon is what brings the transport into being (DaemonStarts).
    facing host: DaemonCredential

    provides:
        DaemonStarts(host)
}

surface BitwardenServerSurface {
    facing _: BitwardenServer
    context connection: ServerConnection

    provides:
        ConnectionInterrupted(connection)
            when connection.status = connected
}

surface AuthenticationSurface {
    facing _: BitwardenServer
    context session: DaemonSession

    provides:
        AuthenticationSucceeded(session, access_token, expires_at, encrypted_organization_key)
            when session.status = authenticating
        AuthenticationRejected(session)
            when session.status = authenticating
        SessionRevoked(session)
            when session.status = active

    @guarantee ServerZeroKnowledge
        -- The organization key crosses the server boundary only in wrapped form: the server
        -- returns encrypted_organization_key (the blob it stored at registration), and the
        -- daemon unwraps it locally with credential.encryption_key, which is never transmitted.
        -- The server holds neither the encryption_key nor the plaintext organization key, and
        -- never any plaintext password the daemon later writes to a cipher.
}

surface IntegrationSurface {
    -- The target-system integration reports the outcome of one rotation try back to the
    -- daemon, keyed by the server-issued attempt_id (the daemon holds no attempt entity).
    -- Best-effort session termination is folded into executing the rotation (ExecuteRotation
    -- guidance), so its outcome rides on the same RotationSucceeded report rather than a
    -- separate surface.
    facing _: Integration

    provides:
        -- The rotation succeeded. session_termination reports the best-effort outcome of
        -- terminating active sessions: not_requested when no termination was asked for,
        -- terminated on success, term_failed when termination was requested but its own
        -- local retries were exhausted (the rotation still succeeded regardless).
        RotationSucceeded(attempt_id, session_termination)
        -- The rotation failed. sync_state reports where the failure left the vault
        -- relative to the target: target_unchanged (the target was not modified; the
        -- stored cipher still matches it), target_updated (the target was changed but
        -- the cipher was not updated -- the stored credential is stale until a later
        -- attempt converges), or indeterminate (the daemon cannot tell whether the
        -- change applied, e.g. a timeout after submission).
        RotationAttemptFailed(attempt_id, reason, sync_state)

    @guarantee VerifiedBeforeSuccess
        -- A rotation is reported succeeded only after the new credential has been
        -- verified against the target system (ExecuteRotation step 4) and the cipher
        -- write has been accepted. An unverified change is never reported as success.

    @guarantee TerminationNeverFailsRotation
        -- Session termination is best-effort and decoupled from rotation success: the
        -- rotation succeeds once the password is rotated, verified and the cipher is synced. A
        -- termination failure is reported as term_failed on a successful rotation, never as
        -- a rotation failure, and never re-runs the rotation.

    @guarantee RotationByAdministrativeReset
        -- Every integration rotates by administrative reset: no step of a rotation may
        -- depend on the CURRENT credential value (the integration authenticates with the
        -- resolver-supplied service credentials, never with the password being rotated).
        -- This is what makes retry-convergence sound: after a target_updated failure (the
        -- target changed, the vault stale), the next attempt's independently generated
        -- fresh password can still be applied. An integration that needed the old
        -- password -- a custom script doing a change-password rather than a reset --
        -- would make such a failure unrecoverable, so custom scripts must honour this
        -- constraint too (see the custom-script open questions).
}

-- Connection lifecycle

rule DaemonStarts {
    when: DaemonStarts(host)
    -- No live connection in any non-terminal state: the daemon holds one transport at a
    -- time. Only a refused (terminal) connection may be left behind, permitting a restart.
    requires: not exists ServerConnection{status: connecting}
    requires: not exists ServerConnection{status: connected}
    requires: not exists ServerConnection{status: disconnected}
    ensures: ServerConnection.created(status: connecting)
    @guidance
        -- Starting the daemon creates its (single) transport to the server, beginning in
        -- connecting; DaemonConnects then authenticates a session over it. The guards
        -- keep one live connection at a time while still allowing a restart after a
        -- terminal refusal -- though a restart with the same rejected credential will
        -- simply be refused again (see the credential re-issuance open question).
}

rule DaemonConnects {
    when: connection: ServerConnection.status becomes connecting
    ensures: DaemonSession.created(
        connection: connection,
        credential: credential,
        status: authenticating
    )
    @guidance
        -- Entering the authenticating state represents presenting the provisioned
        -- credential (client_id + client_secret) to the server. The connection is
        -- signalled ready only once authentication succeeds. On reconnection the
        -- daemon reuses the stored credential without re-prompting.
}

rule HandleAuthenticationSucceeded {
    when: AuthenticationSucceeded(session, access_token, expires_at, encrypted_organization_key)
    requires: session.status = authenticating
    let organization_key = UnwrapOrganizationKey(encrypted_organization_key, session.credential.encryption_key)
    ensures: session.status = active
    ensures: session.access_token = access_token
    ensures: session.expires_at = expires_at
    ensures: session.organization_key = organization_key
    ensures:
        if session.connection.status = connecting:
            ConnectionAuthenticated(connection: session.connection)
    @guidance
        -- The server returns a short-lived access token and the wrapped organization key
        -- (encrypted_organization_key -- the blob it stored at registration). The daemon
        -- unwraps it locally with its credential.encryption_key (UnwrapOrganizationKey) to
        -- get the plaintext org key it holds for the session; the key never crosses the
        -- boundary in the clear (ServerZeroKnowledge). This rule covers BOTH initial
        -- authentication (connection still connecting) and a mid-connection token refresh
        -- (RefreshSession, connection already connected): the access token expires and
        -- refreshes many times within the life of one connection, so the connection-status
        -- guard must not exclude the refresh. Only on initial authentication is the
        -- connection signalled ready (ConnectionAuthenticated -> OpenConnection); a refresh
        -- leaves the already-connected transport untouched.
}

rule HandleAuthenticationRejected {
    when: AuthenticationRejected(session)
    requires: session.status = authenticating
    ensures: session.status = revoked
    ensures: ConnectionRefused(connection: session.connection)
    @guidance
        -- Covers both a rejected initial authentication (connection connecting) and a
        -- rejected mid-connection refresh (credential revoked while the transport was up):
        -- in both cases the credential is no good, the session is revoked and the
        -- connection is terminally refused (RefuseConnection accepts either state).
}

rule OpenConnection {
    when: ConnectionAuthenticated(connection)
    requires: connection.status = connecting
    ensures: connection.status = connected
}

rule RefuseConnection {
    when: ConnectionRefused(connection)
    requires: connection.status in {connecting, connected}
    ensures: connection.status = refused
    @guidance
        -- Authentication was rejected -- a bad or revoked credential. This happens either
        -- during connection establishment (connecting) or when a mid-connection token
        -- refresh is rejected (connected). Either way the connection is terminally
        -- refused: unlike a transient drop (which Reconnect retries with backoff), the
        -- daemon does NOT reconnect, because re-presenting the same rejected credential
        -- would just loop forever. A replacement credential must be provisioned before the
        -- daemon can connect again (see the credential re-issuance open question).
}

rule SessionExpires {
    when: session: DaemonSession.expires_at <= now
    requires: session.status = active
    ensures: session.status = expired
    ensures: session.access_token = null
    ensures: session.expires_at = null
    ensures: session.organization_key = null
    @guidance
        -- The access token has a short lifetime. Once it lapses the daemon must
        -- re-authenticate before it can claim or execute further rotations. The token,
        -- expiry and organization key exist only while the session is active
        -- (when-gated), so leaving active clears them; re-authentication re-derives all
        -- three from the fresh response (HandleAuthenticationSucceeded).
}

rule RefreshSession {
    when: session: DaemonSession.status transitions_to expired
    requires: session.status = expired
    ensures: session.status = authenticating
    @guidance
        -- Re-present the provisioned credential to obtain a fresh access token; the
        -- organization key is re-derived from the new authentication response.
}

rule HandleSessionRevoked {
    when: SessionRevoked(session)
    requires: session.status = active
    ensures: session.status = revoked
    ensures: session.access_token = null
    ensures: session.expires_at = null
    ensures: session.organization_key = null
    @guidance
        -- The server can revoke a daemon's credential (e.g. an admin revokes the
        -- daemon, see rotation-server.allium / RevokeDaemon). The session becomes
        -- revoked; the server closes the underlying connection separately (see
        -- ServerInterruptsConnection). The daemon cannot reconnect until issued a
        -- new credential.
}

rule ServerInterruptsConnection {
    when: ConnectionInterrupted(connection)
    requires: connection.status = connected
    ensures: connection.status = disconnected
}

rule ConnectionDropClosesSessions {
    when: conn: ServerConnection.status transitions_to disconnected
    for session in DaemonSessions where connection = conn and status in {authenticating, active, expired}:
        ensures: CloseSession(session: session)
    @guidance
        -- A dropped transport takes its session with it: whatever state the session was
        -- in, it is closed (terminally) and the reconnected transport authenticates a
        -- fresh one (Reconnect -> DaemonConnects). Without this, the old session would
        -- linger active until its token lapsed -- two active sessions after a fast
        -- reconnect, violating AtMostOneActiveSession -- and would then zombie back
        -- through expired -> authenticating, competing for authentication responses.
}

rule CloseActiveSession {
    when: CloseSession(session)
    requires: session.status = active
    ensures: session.status = closed
    ensures: session.access_token = null
    ensures: session.expires_at = null
    ensures: session.organization_key = null
}

rule CloseIdleSession {
    when: CloseSession(session)
    requires: session.status in {authenticating, expired}
    ensures: session.status = closed
}

rule Reconnect {
    when: connection: ServerConnection.status transitions_to disconnected
    requires: connection.status = disconnected
    ensures: connection.status = connecting
    @guidance
        -- The outline of a reconnect: a dropped transport returns to connecting and
        -- re-authenticates. The mechanics -- re-establishing the socket, keep-alive, and
        -- the exponential backoff between attempts -- belong to SignalR/WebSockets, not
        -- the daemon; this rule does not re-specify them.
}

-- Work acquisition (pull with a push nudge)
--
-- Once connected and authenticated, the daemon pulls work. The server notifies it of
-- claimable jobs (rotation-server.allium / RotationAvailable) -- both when a job is
-- offered and, as a catch-up, when the daemon (re)connects -- and the daemon claims each
-- notified job by invoking the server's RotationWork surface (ClaimRotation). The job
-- reference is opaque to the daemon (like the rest of the snapshot shapes): eligibility
-- (enrolled, connected, assigned, job still claimable) is enforced entirely server-side.
-- Each accepted claim makes the server create a RotationAttempt for this daemon and
-- answer with the work snapshot (rotation-server.allium / RotationClaimed), which the
-- daemon executes (StartRotation).

rule ClaimAvailableRotation {
    when: RotationAvailable(job)
    let session = DaemonSession{status: active}
    requires: exists session
    ensures: ClaimRotation(claimer: credential, job: job)
    @guidance
        -- The daemon claims only while it holds an active (authenticated) session; a
        -- notification arriving while unauthenticated is simply not acted on -- the job
        -- stays pending server-side and a later notification (e.g. the on-connect
        -- catch-up) reaches it again. First claim wins among eligible daemons, so a lost
        -- race is not an error: the server refuses the late claim and this daemon moves on.
        --
        -- RotationAvailable is emitted by the server (rotation-server.allium /
        -- NotifyEligibleDaemons / NotifyClaimableJobsOnConnect) and ClaimRotation is a
        -- server-side surface action (rotation-server.allium / RotationWork) the daemon
        -- invokes as the claiming party; the checker may flag both as cross-file
        -- unreachable triggers (INFO). That is expected and benign for a server <-> daemon
        -- chain.
}

-- Rotation execution
--
-- The daemon is a pure client. On each claim the server answers with a self-contained
-- work snapshot (rotation-server.allium / RotationClaimed) -- the attempt_id, the target
-- system details, the cipher, the account identity, and whether to terminate sessions.
-- The daemon executes one rotation try against that snapshot and reports the outcome back
-- to the server by attempt_id. It holds no attempt entity: all state lives server-side.

rule StartRotation {
    when: RotationClaimed(attempt_id, target_system_id, target_system_name, kind, password_policy, cipher, account_identity, terminate_sessions)
    ensures: ExecuteRotation(
        attempt_id: attempt_id,
        target_system_id: target_system_id,
        kind: kind,
        password_policy: password_policy,
        cipher: cipher,
        account_identity: account_identity,
        terminate_sessions: terminate_sessions
    )
    @guidance
        -- The daemon's claim succeeded (rotation-server.allium / ClaimRotation) and the
        -- server answered it with the work snapshot for the job it claimed, including the
        -- server-owned attempt_id. The daemon starts executing the rotation directly from
        -- that snapshot -- it creates no local record. A retry is another claim, answered
        -- with a fresh snapshot and a fresh attempt_id.
        --
        -- RotationClaimed is emitted by the server (rotation-server.allium / ClaimRotation);
        -- the checker may flag this cross-file chained trigger as an unreachable trigger
        -- (INFO) because no rule in THIS module emits it. That is expected and benign for a
        -- server -> daemon chain.
}

rule ExecuteRotation {
    when: ExecuteRotation(attempt_id, target_system_id, kind, password_policy, cipher, account_identity, terminate_sessions)
    let session = DaemonSession{status: active}
    requires: exists session
    let creds = CredentialResolver(target_system_id)
    -- Steps performed in order:
    -- 1. Resolve connection details for the target via CredentialResolver(target_system_id).
    --    The DEFAULT resolver is environment-variable lookup; Bitwarden Secrets Manager and
    --    a local config file are pluggable, opt-in resolvers that can take priority. The
    --    server holds no connection details; only the daemon does.
    -- 2. Generate a new password satisfying password_policy.
    -- 3. Run the kind integration (entra | mssql | custom_script) to update the account
    --    identified by account_identity (an opaque target-system identifier) in the target
    --    system, using creds.
    -- 4. Verify the rotation: confirm via the kind integration that the account now
    --    authenticates with the new password (e.g. an authentication probe; the per-kind
    --    mechanics are an open question). The rotation is not treated as succeeded until
    --    verification passes; a verification failure that survives the local retries is a
    --    failed attempt with sync_state target_updated (the target was changed but the
    --    vault was not).
    -- 5. Read (GET) the cipher for this attempt, replace its password with the new value
    --    encrypted under session.organization_key (other fields ride along as opaque
    --    ciphertext -- not decrypted), and re-PUT the whole object to the server,
    --    referencing the server-owned attempt_id (today's API has no partial-content
    --    write). The daemon reads only this one cipher -- no general cipher-read scope (see
    --    rotation-server.allium / CipherWriteback, ApiScopedReads). The server authorises
    --    the write against the RotationJob claimed for this attempt -- no separate nonce
    --    (AcceptCipherUpdate) -- and only ever stores ciphertext (ServerZeroKnowledge).
    -- 6. If terminate_sessions, attempt to terminate active sessions for account_identity on
    --    the target system as a best-effort follow-on step (see the @guidance below). The
    --    rotation has already succeeded by this point.
    -- Outcome reported by the IntegrationSurface via RotationSucceeded or RotationAttemptFailed
    -- (a failure carries the vault<->target sync_state it left behind), keyed by attempt_id,
    -- which the daemon relays to the server (ReportOutcomeToServer).
    @guidance
        -- Steps 2 and 5 use the Bitwarden Rust SDK. Cipher writes require an active
        -- session: without the unlocked organization_key the daemon cannot produce
        -- valid ciphertext, so rotation cannot proceed until authentication succeeds.
        -- A rejected write (stale or not-in-flight job) surfaces as RotationAttemptFailed;
        -- the server then makes the job claimable again so a fresh attempt retries it.
        -- Each attempt is independent and starts from step 1: a fresh password is
        -- generated and both the target system and cipher are updated together, so a
        -- previous attempt's partial failure is converged by the next success --
        -- sync_state records the interim divergence. The server
        -- already validated that terminate_sessions implies the target supports session
        -- termination at config time (rotation-server.allium / CreateRotationConfig), so the
        -- daemon never sees a "requested but unsupported" case.
        --
        -- Transient faults are absorbed locally: a step that fails with a transient error
        -- (a network blip, a momentary target-system or API error) is retried in place up to
        -- config.max_retry_attempts with a config.retry_base_delay backoff before the
        -- rotation is reported failed. This keeps a brief glitch from costing a whole server
        -- round-trip. RotationAttemptFailed is emitted only once these local retries are
        -- exhausted (or the error is plainly non-transient); the server's re-claim is the
        -- outer retry for failures that survive them.
        --
        -- Where a failure leaves the vault relative to the target is reported as
        -- sync_state on RotationAttemptFailed: a failure before the target accepted the
        -- change (steps 1-3, with the update known not to have applied) is
        -- target_unchanged; a step-3 failure where the daemon cannot tell whether the
        -- update applied (e.g. a timeout after submission) is indeterminate; a failure
        -- after the target changed -- verification (step 4) or the cipher write (step 5)
        -- -- is target_updated, meaning the stored cipher is stale until a later attempt
        -- or rotation converges.
        --
        -- Session termination (step 6) is part of executing the rotation, NOT a separate
        -- task. It runs only after the rotation has succeeded and only when
        -- terminate_sessions is set. It has its OWN independent local retry loop using the
        -- same config.max_retry_attempts / config.retry_base_delay budget (mirroring the
        -- transient-step retry above), so a termination failure is absorbed locally and,
        -- if its retries are exhausted, NEVER re-runs the rotation. The outcome rides on
        -- the success report's session_termination field: not_requested (terminate_sessions
        -- false), terminated (succeeded), or term_failed (its local retries exhausted).
}

rule ExecuteRotationWithoutSession {
    when: ExecuteRotation(attempt_id, target_system_id, kind, password_policy, cipher, account_identity, terminate_sessions)
    requires: not exists DaemonSession{status: active}
    ensures: RotationAttemptFailed(attempt_id: attempt_id, reason: "no active session", sync_state: target_unchanged)
    @guidance
        -- The session lapsed (or was revoked) between claiming and executing -- rare,
        -- since ClaimAvailableRotation requires an active session, but the token can
        -- expire in the gap. Without the org key the daemon cannot produce valid
        -- ciphertext, so rather than stalling the claimed job until the server's job_ttl
        -- backstop (rotation-server.allium / JobTimesOut), the attempt fails fast with
        -- nothing done (sync_state target_unchanged); the server re-offers the job and a
        -- re-authenticated daemon re-claims it.
}

rule ReportOutcomeToServer {
    when: RotationSucceeded(attempt_id, session_termination)
    ensures: ReportRotationSucceeded(
        reporter: credential,
        attempt_id: attempt_id,
        session_termination: session_termination
    )
    @guidance
        -- The rotation succeeded: the daemon reports the outcome to the server by
        -- attempt_id (rotation-server.allium / RotationOutcomeReporting), carrying the
        -- best-effort session-termination result. The server owns the RotationAttempt and
        -- transitions it on this report (rotation-server.allium / RecordRotationSucceeded).
        --
        -- ReportRotationSucceeded is a server-side surface action (rotation-server.allium /
        -- RotationOutcomeReporting); the daemon, as the reporting party, invokes it. The
        -- checker may flag this cross-file emission as an unreachable trigger (INFO) because
        -- no rule in THIS module consumes it. That is expected and benign for a
        -- daemon -> server call.
}

rule ReportFailureToServer {
    when: RotationAttemptFailed(attempt_id, reason, sync_state)
    ensures: ReportRotationFailed(
        reporter: credential,
        attempt_id: attempt_id,
        failure_reason: reason,
        sync_state: sync_state
    )
    @guidance
        -- The rotation failed (local transient retries exhausted, or a plainly
        -- non-transient error). The daemon reports failed to the server by attempt_id,
        -- carrying the vault<->target sync_state the failure left behind (recorded on the
        -- server-owned attempt, rotation-server.allium / FailureSyncState); the
        -- server records the failure and, if the job has retries left, makes it claimable
        -- again for a fresh attempt -- otherwise it fails the job (rotation-server.allium /
        -- RecordRotationFailed -> RetryJob / FailJob). The daemon itself does not retry the
        -- attempt; it only retries transient sub-steps locally (see ExecuteRotation).
        --
        -- ReportRotationFailed is a server-side surface action; see ReportOutcomeToServer
        -- for the cross-file unreachable-trigger note.
}

-- Invariants

invariant AtMostOneActiveSession {
    for a in DaemonSessions:
        for b in DaemonSessions:
            (a != b and a.status = active) implies b.status != active
}

-- Resolved: the daemon authenticates with a provisioned access token via an OAuth2
-- client-credentials grant, yielding a short-lived, refreshable, revocable session
-- (see DaemonSession, AuthenticationSurface and the auth rules).
-- Resolved (design): the daemon holds the organization key (from authentication) to
-- encrypt cipher writes, but each write is authorised against the server's own in-flight
-- RotationJob for the rotation -- no separate nonce (see rotation-server.allium /
-- AcceptCipherUpdate, CipherWriteback). A write is accepted only for a job that
-- belongs to this daemon, is in-flight and within its window, and targets the job's
-- cipher, so writes are tightly scoped to the specific rotated cipher and window. The
-- read blast radius of holding the org key is contained at the API authorization layer:
-- the daemon's credential has NO general cipher-read scope, so the server never serves it
-- ciphertext beyond the cipher it is actively rotating (see ApiScopedReads). Since today's
-- API has no partial-content write, rotation GETs and re-PUTs that one cipher (full
-- object) -- the read scope is exactly the cipher being rotated.
-- Resolved: the RotationAttempt is server-owned (rotation-server.allium): the server
-- creates it when a daemon claims a job (ClaimRotation) and transitions it on the daemon's
-- outcome report. The daemon holds NO attempt entity; it executes from the work snapshot
-- carried on the claim and reports the outcome by attempt_id (StartRotation,
-- ExecuteRotation, ReportOutcomeToServer / ReportFailureToServer).
-- Resolved: session termination is a best-effort, guidance-level step of executing the
-- rotation (ExecuteRotation), with its own local retry budget; it is no longer a separate
-- entity. Its outcome rides on the success report (RotationSucceeded.session_termination).
open question "Read blast radius (residual): the organization key remains the theoretical crypto blast radius -- a daemon compromise combined with an API-authorization bypass would expose the org vault. Closing it at the crypto layer (so a stolen key alone reveals nothing beyond the managed cipher) needs per-cipher item keys (delivered per attempt, job-bound) or a write-only asymmetric scheme; both depend on Bitwarden per-item cipher keys. Deferred."
open question "Cipher write granularity: with no partial-content API (Option A), rotation re-PUTs the whole cipher, so the in-flight job authorises replacing the entire cipher (not just its password) and requires a read-before-write. A dedicated partial-update / rotate endpoint would remove the read and tighten write scope to the password field. A separate bearer write-token (a field on the job) would add defence in depth against an ownership-check bug. Both deferred."
open question "Credential re-issuance: once a daemon's credential is revoked the session is terminal. How is a replacement credential provisioned and delivered to the daemon?"
open question "Custom script result format: exit code, stdout parsing, or structured output? Exit code is simplest and most portable."
open question "Verification mechanics per kind: step 4 of ExecuteRotation verifies the rotated credential before the rotation is reported succeeded. MSSQL can probe by opening a connection as the rotated login; Entra may not permit a plain password probe (MFA / conditional access can block it) and may need a directory-level confirmation instead; a custom script needs a verify operation in its contract. What does verification concretely mean for each kind, and may a kind opt out?"
open question "Mid-rotation disconnect: when the daemon's connection drops mid-execution, its claim may be released server-side and re-claimed by another daemon while this one is still acting on the target system (the cipher API is fenced; the target system is not). Should the daemon abort in-flight execution on a connection drop? (See the claim-release race question in rotation-server.allium.)"
open question "Credential resolver precedence: the default resolver is environment variables, with Bitwarden Secrets Manager and a local config file as pluggable, opt-in resolvers. When more than one is configured, what is the precedence and is it per-target or daemon-wide? (CredentialResolver encapsulates the lookup; the precedence policy is not yet pinned down.)"
open question "Token expiry mid-rotation: the access token can lapse between claiming a job and the cipher write (ExecuteRotation step 5). ExecuteRotationWithoutSession covers only the claim-to-start gap; mid-execution, should the daemon pause and re-authenticate in place (the local transient-retry budget absorbing the refresh), or fail the attempt with the sync_state it has reached?"
-- Resolved in rotation-server.allium: target systems are defined and managed server-side
-- as the TargetSystem entity (id, name, method, kind, password policy), with no connection
-- details; the daemon resolves those locally by id via CredentialResolver.
-- Resolved in rotation-server.allium: a rotation is tracked server-side as a RotationJob;
-- a job in flight when the claiming daemon's connection drops has its claim released --
-- the same job returns to claimable after a backoff -- and a daemon re-claims it (a
-- fresh attempt) once connected.
